This implementation architecture bridges high-performance offline training in **PyTorch** with ultra-low-latency execution in **WebGPU (WGSL)**. To achieve **<1ms inference**, we bypass traditional buffer-bound tensor operations and instead "bake" the neural network weights directly into the shader source as **constant arrays**. This allows the GPU compiler to optimize the weights into registers or immediate values. ### Part 1: The Python Training & Export Pipeline We define a "TinyNIS" MLP. To keep inference fast, we use a 3-layer architecture. The model learns to map the incoming view vector and surface roughness to a sampling distribution. ```python import torch import torch.nn as nn import numpy as np class TinyNIS(nn.Module): """ Ultra-lightweight MLP for Neural Importance Sampling. Input: [cos_theta_v, phi_v, roughness] Output: [sampled_theta, sampled_phi, confidence] """ def __init__(self): super().__init__() self.net = nn.Sequential( nn.Linear(3, 16), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(16, 16), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(16, 3) # Output: 2D direction + 1D confidence ) def forward(self, x): return self.net(x) def export_to_wgsl(model: nn.Module): """ Transpiles PyTorch weights into WGSL constant arrays. Using row-major storage for predictable indexing in shaders. """ output = "// Neural Importance Sampling - Pre-trained Weights
" for name, param in model.named_parameters(): data = param.detach().cpu().numpy() name_clean = name.replace(".", "_") if "weight" in name: # Flattening for WGSL array rows, cols = data.shape flat_data = ", ".join([f"{x:.6f}f" for x in data.flatten()]) output += f"const {name_clean}: array<f32, {data.size}> = array<f32, {data.size}>(
 {flat_data}
);
" else: # Biases flat_data = ", ".join([f"{x:.6f}f" for x in data.flatten()]) output += f"const {name_clean}: array<f32, {data.size}> = array<f32, {data.size}>({flat_data});
" return output # --- Mock Trai
